{"entries":{"chapter-17-study-community-based-long-term-services-and-support-and-home-based-medical-care|Chapter 17 Study: Community-Based Long-Term Services and Support, and Home-Based Medical Care":["u1"],"summary|Summary":["u2"],"copy|LTSS covers personal, nursing and social support; home-based medical care adds clinician-delivered care. Terminology overlaps (p237).":["u3"],"copy|Most people needing long-term care prefer remaining in the community; unpaid family and friends provide much of the care (p237, p241).":["u4"],"copy|Consider function, cognition, chronic illness, goals, caregiver capacity and local services when selecting a model (p237-241).":["u5"],"copy|Episodic models include skilled home health, transitional care, rehabilitation and hospital at home; longitudinal models include primary care, co-management, integrated medical/social care and PACE (p238-241).":["u6"],"copy|Day care, assisted living and continuing-care communities offer different mixes of housing, supervision and services (p239-241).":["u7"],"copy|Home-based primary care targets complex, often homebound patients; hospital at home substitutes acute hospital-level treatment for selected patients (p238, p243).":["u8"],"copy|PACE integrates medical, social and functional care for eligible nursing-home-level patients; home palliative/hospice care addresses serious illness and symptoms (p238-239).":["u9"],"copy|In the United States, funding varies across Medicare, Medicaid, private payment and local resources; coverage of skilled care differs from ongoing personal assistance (p239-242).":["u10"],"copy|Caregiver work has substantial indirect costs: lost earnings, productivity, health effects and future social-insurance losses (p241).":["u11"],"copy|Reviews of home-based primary care report less emergency/hospital use and improved satisfaction, but models and target populations differ (p243).":["u12"],"copy|Transitional care emphasizes coordination, education, medications and follow-up; community paramedicine offers urgent triage or planned postdischarge support (p243).":["u13"],"copy|Home-based services are most effective when appropriately targeted. Heterogeneity, selection and attrition complicate comparisons (p243-245).":["u14"],"copy|Cost-effectiveness depends on who receives services and the payment system; meeting previously unmet needs may increase service use (p245).":["u15"],"copy|Telemedicine can improve access and outcomes in some situations; study methods and comparisons with in-person care vary (p245).":["u16"],"copy|Broader implementation needs coordinated financing, workforce and technology, while retaining quality and patient-centered goals (p246).":["u17"],"exam-questions|Exam questions":["u18"],"copy|CHAPTER 17s / HAZZARD":["u19"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 17; the book and the full chapter are the reference.":["u20"],"copy|Match home care to need":["u21"],"copy|Delivery and payment":["u22"],"copy|Evidence and implementation":["u23"],"copy|No past-exam questions are currently linked to this chapter.":["u24"],"copy|Sixteen past-exam questions are linked by topic to the Israeli services discussed in this chapter’s teaching notes.":["u24"]},"maxId":24}
